WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.550
The CPU supports tokenization, application logic,

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.949
and inference when layers remain on the processor.

00:00:05.949 --> 00:00:09.040
Core behavior and memory bandwidth influence CPU performance.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.320
CPU-only systems can run useful smaller quantized models but may have lower generation

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:13.820
speed.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.435
A discrete GPU provides parallel compute and dedicated VRAM.

00:00:17.435 --> 00:00:19.775
VRAM must hold model weights, cache, activations,

00:00:19.775 --> 00:00:21.800
and runtime overhead for accelerated work.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.505
The operating system and display may also consume part of the available memory.

00:00:25.505 --> 00:00:29.310
System RAM supports the operating system, applications, model loading,

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.460
CPU-resident layers, and cache.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.430
Unified-memory systems share a pool between CPU and GPU.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.295
Shared capacity can increase flexibility, but bandwidth, thermals,

00:00:37.295 --> 00:00:39.760
and total memory still limit performance.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.580
Storage affects download capacity, startup, model switching, logs, and backups.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:47.560
Keeping several parameter sizes and quantizations can consume substantial space.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.840
Fast storage cannot compensate for insufficient working memory during inference.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.705
Power and cooling determine sustained behavior.

00:00:53.705 --> 00:00:58.040
A laptop may benchmark well briefly and slow under heat or battery limits.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.000
Desktops offer easier cooling and upgrades.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.320
Mini PCs trade compactness for expansion and thermal constraints.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.080
Apple Silicon, NVIDIA, AMD,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.025
and CPU-only systems use different acceleration paths and software support.

00:01:10.025 --> 00:01:13.800
Inventory exact hardware, available memory, operating system, drivers, power mode,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.520
and cooling before using someone else's performance claim.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.560
Use available memory, not the number printed on a product page.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.050
Displays, operating systems, background applications, security tools,

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:26.560
and other services reduce the capacity the runtime can actually allocate.
